It holds that\begin{itemize} \item[(1)]\begin{align}  \Sigma =VDV^t, \end{align}

\item[(2)]\begin{align}  \lambda _{\min }(\Sigma ) \geqslant \min _{\alpha \in \mathcal{C}} \mathbb {E}\! \left[X_{\alpha }T_{\alpha }(X)\right]^2 \sigma ^2_{\min }(V), \end{align}

and 

\item[(3)]\begin{align}  \lambda _{\max }(\Sigma ) \leqslant \max _{\alpha \in \mathcal{C}} \mathbb {E}\! \left[X_{\alpha }T_{\alpha }(X)\right]^2 \sigma ^2_{\max }(V). \end{align}

\end{itemize}

text:
The covariance matrix Sigma equals V times D times the transpose of V. The smallest eigenvalue of Sigma is at least the minimum over alpha in C of the expected value of X alpha times T alpha of X squared, multiplied by the square of the smallest singular value of V. The largest eigenvalue of Sigma is at most the maximum over alpha in C of the expected value of X alpha times T alpha of X squared, multiplied by the square of the largest singular value of V. These bounds relate the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix to expectations and singular values of V. It holds that\begin{itemize} \item[(1)]\begin{align}  \Sigma =VDV^t, \end{align}

\item[(2)]\begin{align}  \lambda _{\min }(\Sigma ) \geqslant \min _{\alpha \in \mathcal{C}} \mathbb {E}\! \left[X_{\alpha }T_{\alpha }(X)\right]^2 \sigma ^2_{\min }(V), \end{align}

and 

\item[(3)]\begin{align}  \lambda _{\max }(\Sigma ) \leqslant \max _{\alpha \in \mathcal{C}} \mathbb {E}\! \left[X_{\alpha }T_{\alpha }(X)\right]^2 \sigma ^2_{\max }(V). \end{align}

\end{itemize}

